model MultipleInheritance

-- Ported from this repo's own bundled USE examples:
-- use-core/src/main/resources/examples/Others/MultipleInheritance/MultipleInheritance.use
-- (its Info.txt: "generalization between several classes without attributes,
-- operations and associations" -- five classes A, B (<A), C (<A),
-- D (<B, C, E), E). That five-class diamond shape is preserved EXACTLY: A is
-- the common ancestor of B and C, D genuinely inherits along two distinct
-- paths back to A (the diamond) plus a third, unrelated parent E. This
-- plugin has zero multiple-inheritance coverage anywhere in its history
-- before this example.
--
-- The original had no attributes/operations/associations at all, so there
-- was nothing for Kodkod to search over and nothing for a query to
-- navigate. Attributes and invariants below are added on top, purely to
-- give -validate a real bounded search space and to let query.cmd
-- demonstrate attribute access reaching D through EVERY one of its three
-- direct superclasses (B, C, E) plus the shared grandparent (A) at the top
-- of the diamond -- the structure itself is untouched.

class A
attributes
  name : String
end

class B < A
attributes
  levelB : Integer
end

class C < A
attributes
  levelC : Integer
end

class D < B, C, E
attributes
  depth : Integer
end

class E
attributes
  tag : String
end

constraints

-- Root-level invariant: every A -- and therefore, by inheritance, every B,
-- C and D as well -- must carry a non-empty name.
--
-- NOTE (discovered empirically, not documented anywhere upstream): this was
-- originally written as `a.name.size() > 0`. `String.size()` is one more
-- OCL operation this plugin's translator has no Kodkod encoding for (the
-- same family of gap as 04-CivilStatus's `substring` finding) -- it logs
-- `ERROR: Cannot transform invariant... OCL operation size is not
-- supported.` and silently drops the invariant from the Kodkod search
-- (though `check -v` still evaluates it correctly afterwards via USE's own
-- interpreter, so the drop is easy to miss). Rewritten below using `<>` on
-- String, which IS supported (string equality/inequality underlies the
-- derived-association FK checks in 03-CompanyERSchema), so this invariant
-- is now a genuine, Kodkod-enforced search constraint again.
context a: A inv NameNotEmpty: a.name <> ''

-- D-specific invariant reaching back along BOTH diamond paths to A: forces
-- the two attributes D inherits from its two different A-descended parents
-- (levelB from B, levelC from C) to actually differ, so no solution can get
-- away with a symmetric/degenerate assignment.
context d: D inv LevelsDiffer: d.levelB <> d.levelC

-- D-specific invariant over its own attribute plus the one inherited from
-- its third superclass E, which sits outside the A/B/C diamond entirely.
-- (Same `.size()` rewrite as NameNotEmpty above, for the same reason.)
context d: D inv DepthMatchesTag: d.depth > 0 and d.tag <> ''
